{"id":"F260","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1A","direction":"against","note":"I checked the page. S4 lanes are $5,778, $7,281 and $9,101 at 2.5B reads per lane. The 25B-300 lane is $2,850, $3,591 and $4,448 at 3.25B reads per lane. The page is undated: the 25B 100- and 200-cycle rows still say 'Coming Nov. 2024'. The 2.6x ratio is correct. It is a mixed signal, not a clean 'against'. It is below Illumina's 3.4x revenue gap, but at the high end of core ratios, the same as MD Anderson's 2.5x. A tail lab converting here would still pay about 62% less per Gb."},
{"id":"F261","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1A","direction":"against","note":"I confirmed the page, last updated 2025-12-11. S4 PE150 is $20,700 for 8–10B reads. The 25B PE150 is $22,500 for 26B reads. The 25B 200-cycle is $18,600 vs the S4 200-cycle at $18,100. Correction: the $4,600 single S4 lane is the 200-cycle price. The 2.4–3.0x per-read ratio is like-for-like (both in clusters). Like F260, this sits closer to the revenue gap than the Calgary and UConn ratios do, so it is only weakly 'against'."},
{"id":"F262","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1A","direction":"supports","note":"Core rates include each core's overhead, so they do not show what Illumina actually charges. The $21,683 benchmark is a GSA distributor price. GSA marks X flow cells up about 1.41–1.47x over list (25B: $24,155 vs $16,480; NCI pays 71% of GSA). So S4 core rates sitting near $21.7K may be well above Illumina's net price, not 'at list'. The claim that the tail is sold without discounts is not supported. The real takeaway is that core S4 rates cluster at $20–23K across 2024–26."},
{"id":"F263","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1B","direction":"against","note":"I verified the prices: S4-300 4-lane flow cell $14,400 (MGB), $20,736 (non-MGB academic), $22,896 (industry); S4-200 2-lane $12,925. The page has no date. The 3.6x spread is a bad comparison: Salk's $52,098 is an old external rate that has since been cut to $39,701, and it carries core markup. MGB's $14,400 is about what you get by applying NCI's 71%-of-GSA ratio, and close to UConn's $15,938. So it looks like a standard academic net price, not proof of large-account leverage. I re-labelled it 'against', weak."},
{"id":"F265","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1B","direction":"against","note":"I re-checked the arithmetic. Q2'26: 747.2K×$158 + 296.7K×$500 = $266.4M vs 1,043.9K×$255 = $266.2M. The fit uses two equations for two unknowns, so the back-tests carry the evidence. Predictions run 1–4% above actuals, which means unit costs fell only slightly. Caveats: cost of product revenue includes fixed and non-reagent costs. The $500 oncology figure mixes tumor exomes into the MRD draws. This fits the known depth-reinvestment pattern (Signatera Genome)."},
{"id":"F266","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1C","direction":"supports","note":"The share calculation is new and correct. Two cautions. First, a 'unit' and a 'test' may be defined differently. Second, the 5–8 TB draw figure understates Gb: per the known data, the one-time tumor exome per new patient carries about 80–90% of Gb per patient-year. Natera's Gb therefore tracks new-patient adds, not draws, and Signatera Genome draws run 10–15x deeper."},
{"id":"F267","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1C","direction":"against","note":"The y/y math checks: 51.9%, 53.4%, 55.4%, 55.2%, 54.7%, 56.4%. It largely restates the known 283K (+56%) and the q/q series in F176. The only new element is the steady y/y rate. MRD draws are low-Gb, so this cuts only weakly against the thesis."},
{"id":"F268","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1C","direction":"against","note":"Confirmed: on the Q1'26 call Natera said it was 'approaching' an annualized run rate of about 200K orders. The 21-gene version launched 5 Jan 2026. Fetal Focus is mainly a reflex test for Horizon carrier-positive patients when the father is unavailable, so it adds tests on women already tested, not new pregnancies. Orders are not the same as tests processed. These tests are already inside the known non-oncology counts (+12.5%), so this does not add volume beyond what is known."},
{"id":"F269","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"1C","direction":"against","note":"Verified in TDLR. The owner is 13011 McCallen Pass Holding LLC, the landlord, with Natera named in the project title. Cost $6.0M, 12,048 sq ft, start 8 Jun 2026, completion 31 Dec 2026. Scope is a remodel plus an extra make-up air unit. The other Austin projects were not checked. Capacity is not volume, and the sequencer vendor is unknown. This adds detail to the known May 2026 Austin facility announcement."},
{"id":"F276","verdict":"reject","strength":"weak","label":"1C","direction":"supports","note":"Duplicate of F189 and D015, which already cover the genome ≈2x exome cost, mostly reagents, and the flat H2 cost per test. The fleet-capex remark is too minor to stand alone."},
{"id":"F280","verdict":"accept","strength":"weak","label":"1C","direction":"supports","note":"The cost per case ($1,373 vs $1,301) and cases (+18%) are already known. The only new part is revenue per case of about $4,261 vs $3,245 (+31%). The prior-year case count is derived. This shows the customer's growth comes from ASP and mix (reimbursement), which is only loosely tied to Illumina Gb."},
{"id":"F286","verdict":"accept","strength":"medium","label":"2B","direction":"against","note":"The Northwestern figure is already known (AVITI ~$10.8–19/Gb vs 25B $3.2–5.0). UChicago, UC Riverside and Salk are new. The output assumptions are reasonable: AVITI HO about 300 Gb, 25B about 940 Gb per lane. This is consistent with D020 (AVITI list ~3x the 25B). UMN (F140) is the counter-example. VITARI is not yet on rate cards."}
